##: {{ux|en|Why is the hose still '''running'''?}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To make a liquid flow; to make liquid flow from or into an object.
##: {{ux|en|You'll have to '''run''' the water a while before it gets hot.}}
##: {{ux|en|Could you '''run''' a bath for me, please?}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become [[liquid]]; to [[melt]].
##* {{quote-book|en|1717|Ovid|Ovid's Metamorphoses in fifteen books. Translated by the most eminent hands. Adorn'd with sculptures|section=Book the Third, The Story of ''Narcissus''|page=92|translator={{w|Joseph Addison}}|origyear=a. 18 A.D.|passage=As Wax dissolves, as Ice begins to '''run''',|url=https://quod.lib.umich.edu/cgi/t/text/pageviewer-idx?cc=ecco;c=ecco;idno=004871123.0001.000;node=004871123.0001.000:6;seq=125;}}
##* {{quote-book|en|1729|{{w|John Woodward (naturalist)|John Woodward}}|An Attempt Towards a Natural History of the Fossils of England||223|The ''Sussex'' ores '''run''' pretty freely in the Fire for Iron-Ores; otherwise they would hardly be worth working.|section=Tome I}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[leak]] or [[spread]] in an undesirable fashion; to [[bleed]] (especially used of [[dye]] or [[paint]]).
